    "image_with_text_aUPwYe": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text_4FfNng": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>For decades, many manufacturers offshored production to reduce labor and material costs. While this strategy delivered short-term savings, it also introduced longer lead times, reduced oversight, and greater exposure to global supply chain disruptions.</p><p>Recent years have underscored these challenges. According to Everstream Analytics, <a href=\"https://www.supplychaindive.com/news/supply-chains-reshoring-decisions-sourcing-manufacturing-china/597596/\" target=\"_blank\"><strong>98% of global supply chains experienced disruption</strong></a>, leading to shortages, shipping delays, and rising costs across industries. As a result, manufacturers across sectors have begun reassessing where and how their products are made.</p><p>Reshoring initiatives continue to gain momentum as companies look to strengthen supply chain reliability, improve quality control, and better support long-term business growth.</p>",
            "subtext_color": true
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "text_4FfNng"
      ],
      "name": "t:sections.image-with-text.name",
      "settings": {
